tel 1.0

base stenography
import soundness

info   Scala types rendered as the source text a user would write, abbreviated against the imports in scope
detail
    _Stenography_ turns a Scala type into the text a programmer would have written for it at a
    given point in their code: `Int | String & Double`, `Addable by Int to Double`,
    `(e: Enumeration) => e.Value`, `Text^{alpha}`. A compiler's own rendering of a type is
    fully qualified, dealiased and full of encodings — `scala.collection.immutable.List`,
    `Addable { type Operand = Int; type Result = Double }`, `ImpureFunction1` — which is the
    wrong thing to put in an error message, a generated source file, a completion or a
    diagnostic. Stenography writes the type back in its source form, qualifying each name only
    as far as the imports in scope require, and preferring the infix and arrow notations the
    language offers.

    The simplest use is the `Syntax.name` macro, which renders a type against the imports at
    the point where it is called:

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    Syntax.name[Int | String & Double]                     // t"Int | String & Double"
    Syntax.name[(e: Enumeration) => e.Value]               // t"(e: Enumeration) => e.Value"
    Syntax.name[Addable { type Operand = Int }]            // t"Addable by Int"
    Syntax.name[scala.collection.immutable.List[Int]]      // t"collection.immutable.List[Int]"
    ```

    Rendering happens in two steps, so that the second can be repeated against different
    scopes. First, `Syntax(repr)` converts a compiler `TypeRepr` into a `Syntax` tree, which
    depends only on the type: each named entity in it is a `Designator`, a path of term and
    type selections such as `scala.collection.immutable.List`. Second, `Syntax#text` writes the
    tree as text against an `Imports`, which says which scopes are imported (so their members
    are written unqualified), which types are reachable by their leaf name through an `export`,
    which infix type aliases may be used, and whether pure functions are enabled (so which
    arrow, `->` or `=>`, an unannotated function type is written with). The same tree written
    under `Imports.empty` is fully qualified; `Syntax#qualified` is that shorthand.

    The library covers the whole type grammar of Scala 3: unions and intersections with
    minimal parentheses, applied, projected, refined and structural types, named and unnamed
    tuples, function, context-function, dependent and polymorphic function types, type
    lambdas, match types, by-name types, singleton and constant types, wildcards with bounds,
    `into` types, recursive types (whose binder is given a name by `Bindings`), skolems, and
    capture sets with every refinement of a capability (`*`, `.rd`, `.only[…]`,
    `.except[…]`).

    A `Syntax` tree is built inside a macro, since a `TypeRepr` exists only where a `Quotes`
    does; it can then be carried out of the macro, written in several scopes, or inspected as
    an ordinary enum. Libraries which explain errors in terms of types (frontier's
    missing-given diagnostics, spectacular's `Showable` fallback, hyperbole's tree viewer) call
    `stenography.internal.name` from their own macros, and a REPL which renders types outside
    any macro builds its `Imports` with `Imports.resolve`.

    The library has a single source set, `stenography.core`, which depends on the Scala
    compiler itself (`dotty.tools.dotc`) and so is only for use in macros and tools which run
    alongside a compiler. `Bindings`, `Designator`, `Imports` and `Syntax` are exported to the
    `soundness` umbrella.

refer stenography.Syntax
refer stenography.Designator
refer stenography.Imports
refer stenography.Bindings

entry .Syntax   a Scala type as a tree in its source form, written as text against an `Imports`
  detail
      A `Syntax` is the source form of a type, as an enum whose cases are the shapes of Scala's
      type grammar. It is built from a compiler `TypeRepr` by `Syntax(repr)` inside a macro,
      and depends only on the type; `#text` writes it against an `Imports`, which decides how
      far each name is qualified, which infix aliases are used and which function arrow is
      written. The macro `Syntax.name[T]` does both steps at once, against the imports where
      it is called.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Syntax.name[Text is Showable]                            // t"Text is Showable"
      Syntax.name[(Text, Double) ?=> Int]                      // t"(Text, Double) ?=> Int"
      Syntax.name[[T] =>> Option[T]]                           // t"[T] =>> Option[T]"
      Syntax.name[(Addable { type Q = Int })#Result]           // t"(Addable { type Q = Int })#Result"
      ```

      A tree may also be built by hand, which is how code outside a macro writes a type
      known only by name:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val list = Designator.member(Designator(t"scala.collection.immutable"), t"List")
      val text = Designator.member(Designator(t"anticipation"), t"Text")
      val syntax = Syntax.Application(Syntax.Simple(list), List(Syntax.Simple(text)), false)

      syntax.qualified   // t"scala.collection.immutable.List[anticipation.Text]"
      ```

      The same tree is abbreviated further as more of its scopes are imported:

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label Nothing imported: every name is fully qualified
        code
            locally:
              given Imports = Imports.empty
              syntax.text   // t"scala.collection.immutable.List[anticipation.Text]"
      frame
        label The `scala` package imported, as it always is in source
        code
            locally:
              given Imports =
                Imports(scala.collection.immutable.Set(Designator(t"scala")), scala.collection.immutable.Set())
              syntax.text   // t"collection.immutable.List[anticipation.Text]"
      frame
        label `anticipation` imported too: `Text` loses its prefix
        code
            locally:
              given Imports =
                Imports
                  ( scala.collection.immutable.Set(Designator(t"scala"), Designator(t"anticipation")),
                    scala.collection.immutable.Set() )
              syntax.text   // t"collection.immutable.List[Text]"
      frame
        label `scala.collection.immutable` imported: the type as a user would write it
        code
            locally:
              given Imports =
                Imports
                  ( scala.collection.immutable.Set
                      ( Designator(t"scala"),
                        Designator(t"anticipation"),
                        Designator(t"scala.collection.immutable") ),
                    scala.collection.immutable.Set() )
              syntax.text   // t"List[Text]"
      ##
      ```

      The cases of the enum, each a node of the tree:

      - `Simple(designator)`, a named type, written as `designator.text`, and
        `Value(designator)`, a singleton type of a stable path, written with `.type`.
      - `Symbolic(text)` and `Primitive(text)`, text written verbatim: a type parameter, a
        capture root, `?`, a constant type such as `42L` or `"hello"`.
      - `Application(left, elements, infix)`, an applied type, `List[Int]`; where `infix` is
        set (the type constructor is declared `infix`) and its name is reachable unqualified,
        it is written `A op B` instead.
      - `Infix(left, middle, right)`, a binary operator type: `|`, `&`, `is`, `<:`, `>:`, `=>`
        and `=>>` between type parameters and a body, and any infix alias.
      - `Prefix(middle, right)` and `Suffix(left, suffix)`: `into T`, the by-name types
        `=> T` and `-> T`, `case P => R`, a repeated `T *`, a flexible `T?`, a reach
        capability `c*` and a read-only `c.rd`.
      - `Projection(base, text)`, a type projection `A#B`, parenthesising a refined or infix
        base; and `Selection(left, right)`, a path selection `a.B`.
      - `Structural(syntax, types, terms)`, a refinement `Play { type A = X; def f: Y }`, whose
        alias members may be written with an infix alias instead: `Play { type Form = Json }`
        as `Play in Json`. A member which only bounds its type, `type A <: X`, stays a
        `Declaration` and is never written with an alias.
      - `Named(isUsing, name, syntax)`, a named parameter or named-tuple element, `x: Int`, or
        `using x: Int`.
      - `Sequence(style, syntaxes)`, a comma-separated list in `(`, `[` or `{` brackets: a
        tuple, type parameters, a capture set.
      - `Declaration(method, syntaxes, result)`, a member's signature, `(x: Int): String`, as
        `Syntax.signature` makes it.
      - `Compound(syntaxes)`, several trees written one after another.
      - `Match(scrutinee, cases)`, a match type, written on one line as
        `T match { case A => B; case C => D }`.
      - `Capturing(base, refs)`, a type with a capture set: `T^{a, b}`, `T^{}` for an empty
        `refs`, and `T^` for `Unset`, the universal set.
      - `Function(parameters, contextual, impure, refs, result)`, a function type, whose arrow
        records whether it is a context function (`?=>`), whether it is impure (`=>` rather
        than `->`), and its capture set (`->{a}`).

      The cases are ordinary enum cases, so a tree can be matched, rewritten and rebuilt, as
      delicious does to substitute placeholders in a rendered diagnostic.
  refer stenography.Designator
  refer stenography.Imports
  refer stenography.Bindings
  entry .name   `Syntax.name[T]`: a type rendered as source text against the imports where it is called
      A macro which renders the type `T` (of any kind, so a type constructor or type lambda
      may be given) as the text a user would write at the call site. The imports it renders
      against are `scala` and `scala.Predef`, every import in scope at the call (including
      those a REPL has accumulated over earlier lines), the enclosing compilation unit's
      package and its top-level imports. Within those scopes it also collects the targets of
      `export` aliases, so that a type re-exported through `soundness` is written by its leaf
      name under `import soundness.*`, and the infix type aliases which refine a single type
      member, so that `Addable { type Operand = Int }` is written `Addable by Int` where `by`
      is in scope. An unannotated function type is written with `->` where pure functions are
      enabled at the call site, and with `=>` where they are not.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Syntax.name[Addable { type Operand = Int; type Result = Double }]   // t"Addable by Int to Double"
      Syntax.name[Addable { type Operand <: Int }]    // t"Addable { type Operand = ? <: Int }"
      Syntax.name[(name: String, age: Int)]           // t"(name: String, age: Int)"
      Syntax.name[Enumeration#Value]                  // t"Enumeration#Value"
      Syntax.name[None.type]                          // t"None.type"
      ```

      The result is a literal `Text`, computed while the code compiles. Within one
      compilation run the trees of types already rendered and the summaries of the scopes
      searched are cached, so the cost of rendering many types against a large scope is paid
      once.
  entry .apply   `Syntax(repr)`: a compiler type converted to a `Syntax` tree, inside a macro
      Takes a `quotes.reflect.TypeRepr`, with a `Quotes` and a `Bindings` in scope (a fresh
      `Bindings()` is the default), and returns its tree. The tree depends only on the type,
      not on where it will be written: no name is abbreviated, no infix alias is substituted
      and no arrow is chosen until `#text`. Results are cached in the `Bindings`, keyed by the
      type, so a tree converted twice with one `Bindings` is built once. A type of a shape the
      method does not recognise is retried as its type symbol's reference, and otherwise
      becomes `Primitive("<unknown>")`, so conversion never fails.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import scala.quoted.*

      def rendered[T: Type](using Quotes): Text =
        Syntax(quotes.reflect.TypeRepr.of[T]).qualified
      ```

      A skolem, the compiler's `?1` for the unstable argument of a dependent method, is
      written as the type it stands for, and a type member selected from one as the member's
      alias where that type aliases it, or as a projection on the type where it only bounds
      it.
  entry .term   the `Designator` of a term reference, inside a macro
      Converts a `quotes.reflect.TermRef` with `Syntax(…)` and returns the designator of the
      `Value` it becomes, such as the designator of a package or object named in an import.
      Panics if the reference does not convert to a `Value`.
  entry .signature   a member's declared type as a `Syntax.Declaration`, for writing a member's signature
      Takes a member's name and its info as a `TypeRepr`, with a `Quotes` and `Bindings` in
      scope. A method type becomes its parameter list and result, `(x: Int): String`; a
      parameterless or by-name member just its result, `: Int`; a bounded type member its
      bounds, `? <: Int`; and a type lambda its type parameters and body. The result's
      `method` flag says whether it is a term member (`def`) or a type member (`type`).
  entry .clause   a parameter clause as source text: `(x: Int, y: Text)`, `(using Play)` or `[T <: U: Bound]`
      Renders one `quotes.reflect.ParamClause`. For a term clause, each parameter is written
      with its name and type, `inline` where it is declared so, and with `using` before a
      contextual parameter when `showUsing` is set; an anonymous contextual parameter is
      written as its type alone, and a single unnamed one without parentheses. Parameters
      named `evidence$…`, the desugaring of context bounds, are omitted, since they are
      written as bounds on the type parameters instead. For a type clause, each parameter is
      written with its bounds and with the context bound given for its name in `context`,
      the map `.contextBounds` makes.
  entry .contextBounds   the context bounds of a method, recovered from its `evidence$` parameters
      Takes a method's parameter clauses and returns a map from each type parameter's name to
      the bound it was declared with: `T is Showable` or `Showable[T]` becomes the bound
      `Showable` on `T`, and several bounds on one parameter become a `{A, B}` sequence. It is
      what `.clause` needs to write `[T: Showable]` rather than an `evidence$1` parameter.
  entry .typeBounds   a type with its bounds, `T <: U`, `T >: L` or both, omitting those which are trivial
      Takes the `Syntax` of the bounded name and its lower and upper bounds. A lower bound of
      `Nothing` and an upper bound of `Any` are left out, equal bounds are written as the
      type they fix, and a capture-set parameter, which the compiler encodes as the bounds
      `>: CapSet <: CapSet^`, is written as it was declared, `cap^`, with only its nontrivial
      bounds: `cap^ >: {alpha}`.
  entry .symbolic   a name as a `Syntax.Symbolic`, without the `_$` prefix of a compiler-synthesised name
      For a type parameter or other bare name read from the compiler, whose synthesised form
      such as `_$1` should not reach the user as written.
  entry .Space   the `Syntax.Symbolic` for a single space, for assembling token sequences
  entry .Colon   the `Syntax.Symbolic` for `: `, between a name and its type
  entry .Comma   the `Syntax.Symbolic` for `, `, between the elements of a list
  entry .Open   the `Syntax.Symbolic` for `(`, opening a parameter list or tuple
  entry .Close   the `Syntax.Symbolic` for `)`, closing a parameter list or tuple
  entry .OpenType   the `Syntax.Symbolic` for `[`, opening a type parameter or argument list
  entry .CloseType   the `Syntax.Symbolic` for `]`, closing a type parameter or argument list
  entry .Plus   the `Syntax.Symbolic` for `+`, a covariance marker
  entry .Minus   the `Syntax.Symbolic` for `-`, a contravariance marker
  entry #text   the tree written as source text, qualified and abbreviated as an `Imports` directs
      Needs an `Imports` in scope. Each `Designator` is written as `Designator#text` writes
      it, unqualified where it or its owner is imported; an infix type constructor is written
      `A op B` where its name is reachable unqualified; a refinement member which an infix
      alias in `Imports#aliases` refines is written with that alias; and a function type with
      no capture set is written `->` where `Imports#pureFuns` is set, and `=>` where it is
      not, as is a pure by-name type.

      Parentheses are added only where precedence requires them, by `#precedence`: `|` binds
      loosest, then `&`, then the arrows, and a function type to the left of an arrow is
      parenthesised where one to the right is not, since function types associate to the
      right. Alphabetic infix operators such as `is` and `by` score lowest of all, so a
      function type whose parameter is one is written `(Addable by Int) => String`, with
      parentheses Scala would not strictly need.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val function = Syntax.Function
        ( Syntax.Simple(Designator(t"scala#Int")), false, false, Nil,
          Syntax.Simple(Designator(t"anticipation#Text")) )

      val scope = scala.collection.immutable.Set(Designator(t"scala"), Designator(t"anticipation"))
      val none = scala.collection.immutable.Set[Designator]()

      function.text(using Imports(scope, none))                                         // t"Int -> Text"
      function.text(using Imports(scope, none, scala.collection.immutable.Map(), false))  // t"Int => Text"
      ```
  entry #qualified   the tree written fully qualified: `#text` against `Imports.empty`
      Every name with its full path, no infix aliases, and pure functions assumed, so
      `->` and `=>` stay distinct. The form for a log, a cache key or a comparison, where
      the reader's imports are unknown.
  entry #precedence   how tightly the node binds, from 0 (loosest) to 10, deciding where `#text` puts parentheses
      Named types, applications, sequences and other atoms are 10; a projection or capturing
      type 9; an infix operator type scores by its operator's first character as Scala's
      operators do (`|` 1, `^` 2, `&` 3, `=` and `!` 4, `<` and `>` 5, `:` 6, `+` and `-` 7,
      `*`, `/` and `%` 8, a letter 0); a function type 4; and a refinement, prefix, suffix or
      named node 0, so it is parenthesised wherever it is enclosed.

entry .Designator   a path to a named type or term, such as `scala.collection.immutable.List`, as a tree of selections
  detail
      A `Designator` names a type or a term by the path of selections which reaches it from a
      top-level name: `Top(name)` for the first segment, `Term(parent, name)` for a term
      selected from its parent, and `Type(parent, name)` for a type selected from it. A
      `Syntax.Simple` or `Syntax.Value` holds one, and `#text` writes it against an `Imports`,
      leaving out as much of the path as the imports make unnecessary.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val list = Designator.member(Designator(t"scala.collection.immutable"), t"List")
      list.qualified                     // t"scala.collection.immutable.List"
      list.parent                        // the designator of `scala.collection.immutable`
      list.companionObject.qualified     // t"scala.collection.immutable.List", as a term

      Designator(t"immutable.List#Value") ==
        Designator.Term(Designator.Type(Designator.Top(t"immutable"), t"List"), t"Value")
      ```

      In the text form `Designator(text)` reads, a segment followed by `.` is a term and one
      followed by `#` is a type, as Scala writes a projection, `List#Value`; the last segment
      is always a term, so a type is made from its parent with `.member`. `#text` writes the
      same separators.

      Every designator made through `.top`, `.term`, `.member`, `.apply`, `#child`,
      `#companionObject` and `#companionType` is interned, so equal designators made that way
      are the same instance and compare by reference first. Equality is still structural, and
      a designator built with the case constructors equals the interned one; the hash is
      computed once. The intern table holds its entries weakly, so it keeps no designator
      alive.
  refer stenography.Syntax
  refer stenography.Imports
  entry .apply   `Designator(text)`: a designator parsed from its dotted, `#`-separated text form
      The first segment becomes a `Top`, each segment followed by `.` a `Term` and each
      followed by `#` a `Type`; the last segment is a `Term`. `Designator(t"scala")` is the
      designator of the `scala` package, as an `Imports` names an imported scope.
  entry .top   a top-level designator, `Top(name)`, interned
  entry .term   a term selected from a parent designator, `Term(parent, name)`, interned
  entry .member   a type selected from a parent designator, `Type(parent, name)`, interned
      The way to make the designator of a type such as `List` within the package
      `scala.collection.immutable`, since `Designator(text)` makes its last segment a term.
  entry #name   the last segment of the path: `List` for `scala.collection.immutable.List`
  entry #parent   the designator this one is selected from, or `Unset` for a `Top`
  entry #child   a designator selected from this one: a type if `isType`, otherwise a term
  entry #companionObject   the term of the same name as a type designator, or the designator itself if it is not a type
  entry #companionType   the type of the same name as a term designator, or the designator itself if it is not a term
  entry #qualified   the full path as text, `scala.collection.immutable.List`: `#text` against `Imports.empty`
  entry #text   the shortest form of the path an `Imports` allows
      Needs an `Imports` in scope. The designator is written by its name alone if
      `Imports#direct` holds it (it is reachable through an `export`) or if its parent is
      imported; otherwise its parent is written, recursively, followed by `#` if the parent
      is a type and `.` if it is a term, and then the name. So with `scala` imported,
      `scala.collection.immutable.List` is written `collection.immutable.List`.
  entry #symbol   the separator between this designator's parent and its name
      `typeSymbol` (default `#`) if the parent is a type, `termSymbol` (default `.`) if it is a
      term or a `Top`, and empty text for a `Top`, which has no parent.
  entry #render   the full path with `⌗` after a type, for display where `#` would be misread
      As `#qualified`, but with the selection of a member from a type written `⌗` rather
      than `#`, for a rendering which must not be confused with a Scala projection or a URL
      fragment.

entry .Imports   which names are in scope where a type is written: imported scopes, exports, infix aliases, arrows
  detail
      An `Imports` is what `Syntax#text` and `Designator#text` consult to decide how to write a
      type at a particular point in source code. It is a case class of four fields:

      - `#designators`, the scopes whose members are reachable unqualified: the designators of
        the packages and objects wildcard-imported, and of the enclosing package.
      - `#direct`, designators reachable by their leaf name although their owner is not
        imported, as the target of an `export` alias is: `jacinta.Json` is written `Json`
        under `import soundness.*` because `soundness` exports it.
      - `#aliases`, a map from the name of a refined type member to the infix type alias in
        scope which refines it, `"Form" -> t"in"`, so that `Play { type Form = Json }` can be
        written `Play in Json`.
      - `#pureFuns`, whether the code being written to has pure functions enabled, which
        capture checking implies. It decides whether an unannotated function type is written
        `->` or `=>`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val refined = Syntax.Structural
        ( Syntax.Simple(Designator(t"Addable")),
          Ledger((t"Operand", Syntax.Simple(Designator(t"Int")))),
          Ledger() )

      val none = scala.collection.immutable.Set[Designator]()

      refined.text(using Imports(none, none))                                                    // t"Addable { type Operand = Int }"
      refined.text(using Imports(none, none, scala.collection.immutable.Map("Operand" -> t"by")))  // t"Addable by Int"
      ```

      Inside a macro, `Syntax.name` and `stenography.internal.name` build the `Imports` of the
      call site themselves. Code which renders types outside any macro — a REPL abbreviating
      a diagnostic against its session's imports, a documentation tool — must build one by
      hand, and should do so with `.resolve` rather than the constructor, since only then are
      the exports and infix aliases of each imported scope included. The fields are standard
      library `Set` and `Map`s, written `scala.collection.immutable.Set`, since they are
      shared with compiler code.
  refer stenography.Syntax
  refer stenography.Designator
  entry .empty   no scopes, exports or aliases, with pure functions: everything fully qualified
      The `Imports` which `Syntax#qualified` and `Designator#qualified` write against.
  entry .resolve   an `Imports` of imported scopes, extended by each scope's exports and infix aliases
      `Imports.resolve(designators, direct)` is `Imports(designators, direct)` with `direct`
      extended by `.exports` of every one of `designators`, and `aliases` filled from
      `.infixAliases` of each, so that a type reached through a wildcard-imported prelude
      such as `soundness` renders as the user would write it. It needs the compiler's
      `dotty.tools.dotc.core.Contexts.Context`, and that context must have the quote cache
      installed, as a macro's context does and as `delicious.Reifier` installs on a
      standalone one. Where two scopes offer an infix alias for the same member, the alias
      first by name is chosen, so the result does not depend on the order of the scopes.
  entry .exports   the designators reachable by their leaf name through the `export` aliases of a scope
      Takes the designator of a package or object, such as `Designator(t"soundness")`, and
      a compiler `Context`, and returns the target of every exported type alias declared in
      it, together with that target's companion: under `import soundness.*`, `Textual` and
      `Textual.text` are both written unqualified. Only an alias which renames its target, or
      applies it to exactly its own type parameters in order, counts; an alias which reshapes
      its parameters (a tuple type, `Some`, `<:<`) does not make its target reachable by
      name. A scope which does not resolve, or whose declarations cannot be read, contributes
      nothing, since rendering must never throw.
  entry .infixAliases   the infix type aliases declared in a scope which each refine a single type member
      Takes a scope's designator and a compiler `Context`, and returns a map from the refined
      member's name to the alias: for `infix type in[refined, form] = refined { type Form =
      form }`, the pair `"Form" -> t"in"`. The alias must take exactly two type parameters,
      the first as the refinement's parent and the second as the member's alias; any other
      infix alias has no two-operand form and is skipped. Where two aliases in the scope
      refine the same member, the one first by name wins. Never throws.
  entry #designators   the scopes whose members are written unqualified
  entry #direct   the designators written by their leaf name, as the targets of `export` aliases are
  entry #aliases   the infix type alias to write for each refined member name, as `"Form" -> t"in"`
  entry #pureFuns   whether pure functions are enabled where the type is written, so `->` is distinct from `=>`
      With it set (the default, and the case wherever capture checking is on), a function
      type with no capture set is written `A -> B` and an impure one `A => B`. With it unset,
      every function arrow is written `=>`, since `A => B` then means the unannotated
      `FunctionN`.
  entry #has   whether a designator is one of the imported scopes in `#designators`
  entry #hasDirect   whether a designator is in `#direct`, reachable by its leaf name

entry .Bindings   the names given to recursive types' binders, and the cache of trees, shared while types are converted
  detail
      `Syntax(repr)` takes a `Bindings` as a contextual parameter. It holds two things which
      depend on nothing but the types converted: the name given to the binder of each
      recursive type, so that a self-reference in a refinement is written consistently, and a
      cache of the trees already built. Nothing in it depends on where a tree will be written,
      so one `Bindings` may be shared by every conversion in a compilation run, as
      `stenography.internal.name` shares one; `Bindings()` makes a fresh one, and is the
      default when none is given.

      Since the names are shared, the binder of a recursive type may be numbered, `self2`,
      where a type converted earlier with the same `Bindings` used the same candidate name.
  refer stenography.Syntax
  entry #nameFor   the name for a binder, the candidate or a numbered variant, the same each time it is asked
      Takes the binder, compared by reference, and a by-name candidate, derived from the
      recursive type's base type with its first letter lowered. The first binder to ask for a
      candidate is given it as it is, and later ones the candidate followed by `2`, `3`, ….
      Asking again for the same binder returns the name it was first given.

entry .internal   the macro-side entry points: a type rendered against the imports at a macro's expansion site
  detail
      `internal.name` is what a library's own macro calls to render a type for its users: it
      is how frontier writes the types in a missing-given diagnostic, how spectacular's
      `Showable` names a type it cannot show, and how hyperbole labels a tree. It does
      everything `Syntax.name` does, but takes the type as a `TypeRepr` or a `Type` within an
      existing macro rather than as a type argument at the call site.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import scala.quoted.*

      def explain[T: Type](using Quotes): Text = t"no instance for ${stenography.internal.name[T]}"
      ```

      The imports are those at the macro's expansion site, gathered as `Syntax.name` gathers
      them, with the exports and infix aliases of every wildcard-imported scope and of the
      unit's own package. Within one compilation run, the converted trees, each scope's
      summary and the union of the summaries for each list of scopes are cached, one cache
      per compiler, holding only the latest run and held softly so that a discarded compiler
      is not kept alive. Outside a compiler-backed `Quotes`, `name` falls back to the
      imports alone.
  refer stenography.Syntax
  entry .name   a type rendered as source text against the imports at the current macro expansion
      Takes a `quotes.reflect.TypeRepr`, or a type argument with a `Quotes` in scope, and
      returns the text `Syntax.name` would have produced at the expansion site.
  entry .designator   the macro behind `Syntax.name`: the rendered type as a `Text` literal
